   {
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    "mainEntity": [
     {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Can a hair transplant trigger cancerous growths on the scalp?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
       "@type": "Answer",
       "text": "No. A hair transplant does not trigger cancerous growths on the scalp. Hair transplant involves moving your own hair follicles from one part of the scalp to another. It does not introduce cancer cells or alter your DNA. However, if you already have an unusual scalp lesion, mole, non-healing wound, or suspicious growth, it should be examined by a dermatologist before any procedure."
      }
     },
     {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Is it true that I can never shave my head or cut my hair after a transplant?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
       "@type": "Answer",
       "text": "No. Once healing is complete, you can cut, trim, style, and even shave your hair. In the early healing phase, you must follow specific instructions to protect the grafts. But after proper healing, transplanted hair behaves like your natural hair."
      }
     },
     {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Do all cheap clinics in Bangalore use the same technology as premium centers?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
       "@type": "Answer",
       "text": "No. The word “FUE” or “advanced technique” does not guarantee the same quality everywhere. The final outcome depends on doctor involvement, graft handling, hairline design, OT hygiene, team training, implantation angle, donor management, and aftercare. A low-cost clinic may reduce cost by compromising on time, staff, sterilization, planning, or doctor supervision. Always verify before choosing."
      }
     },
     {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How can I personally verify if a myth I read online about hair restoration is true or false?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
       "@type": "Answer",
       "text": "Ask four questions: Is the information coming from a qualified dermatologist or hair transplant surgeon? Is the claim medically explained or only emotionally dramatic? Does the clinic or person openly discuss risks and limitations? Can the claim be confirmed during an in-person consultation? If something sounds extremely scary or unbelievably perfect, verify it with a qualified doctor."
      }
     },
     {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Can transplanted hair fall out after surgery?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
       "@type": "Answer",
       "text": "Yes, the visible transplanted hair shafts often shed in the first few weeks. This is normal. The follicle remains under the skin and later starts producing new hair. Early growth usually starts around 3 to 4 months, with fuller results developing over 9 to 12 months."
      }
     },
     {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Is hair transplant painful?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
       "@type": "Answer",
       "text": "Most patients tolerate the procedure well because it is done under local anaesthesia. You may feel injections at the beginning and mild soreness after the procedure, but severe pain is uncommon when the surgery and aftercare are properly managed."
      }
     },
     {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Can women undergo hair transplant?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
       "@type": "Answer",
       "text": "Yes, selected women can undergo hair transplant. But the cause of hair loss must be diagnosed properly first. Female hair loss can be due to genetic thinning, hormonal imbalance, nutritional deficiency, thyroid issues, traction hairstyles, or other scalp conditions. The treatment plan must be individualized."
      }
     },
     {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Is hair transplant better than medicines?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
       "@type": "Answer",
       "text": "They serve different purposes. Medicines like minoxidil or finasteride help slow ongoing hair loss and protect existing hair. Hair transplant restores hair in areas where follicles are already lost or severely miniaturized. Many patients need both for the best long-term result."
      }
     },
     {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Can I wear a helmet after hair transplant?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
       "@type": "Answer",
       "text": "Not immediately. You should avoid pressure, friction, and sweating in the early healing period. Your surgeon will guide you on when it is safe to resume helmet use, depending on your healing and procedure details. This is especially important for patients commuting across Bangalore on two-wheelers."
      }
     },
     {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Is one hair transplant enough for life?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
       "@type": "Answer",
       "text": "For some patients, one session may be enough. For others, future sessions may be needed if hair loss progresses or if larger areas need coverage. A responsible surgeon will plan according to your current hair loss and future risk."
      }
     },
     {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Can a failed hair transplant be repaired?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
       "@type": "Answer",
       "text": "In many cases, yes. Repair may involve redesigning the hairline, adding density, removing wrongly placed grafts, improving unnatural angles, or using beard/body hair in selected cases. However, repair is usually more complex than a first transplant. That is why choosing the right clinic the first time matters."
      }
     },
     {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the biggest myth about hair transplant?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
       "@type": "Answer",
       "text": "The biggest myth is that hair transplant is either dangerous or magical. It is neither. It is a safe and effective medical procedure when done by a qualified team on the right candidate with realistic expectations. The goal is not just to grow hair. The goal is to restore confidence safely, naturally, and responsibly."
      }
     }
    ],
    "@id": "https://neofollicletransplant.com/hair-transplant-myths-vs-facts/#faq_hair_transplant_myths_vs_facts"
   }
